/// TodoMVC spike WORLDS — polymorph-apps#16 (app execution model) and #15
/// (WIT shape rules) drive every choice here.
///
/// THIS FILE DEFINES NO INTERFACES. It used to define `dom`, `events` and
/// `shell` inline, as a copy of the framework's surface contract. That copy
/// went stale: #74 ("Split the WIT contracts", 2026-08-21) moved the real
/// definitions to the repo root as `polyvisor:surface@0.1.0` and moved the
/// host onto those import keys, while this file kept saying
/// `polymorph:todomvc-spike@0.0.1` — so every guest built from it asked for
/// keys the host no longer registered, and none of them could instantiate.
/// See README.md's "Known-broken" entry for the full record.
///
/// The fix is for the spike to STOP OWNING A SECOND DEFINITION. `deps/
/// polyvisor-surface` is a SYMLINK to `<repo>/wit/surface`, not a vendored
/// copy: the surface lives in this same repository, so there is no reason
/// for a snapshot that can drift again. What remains here is what the spike
/// genuinely owns — its two worlds.
///
/// The interfaces those worlds import are documented at their definition
/// (`../../../wit/surface/surface.wit`): `dom` is a WebIDL-mirror subset,
/// `events` and `shell` are framework-shaped (subscription tokens and
/// record-shaped event delivery — #15 rule 5 — plus the capability grant,
/// where `root` hands the app its subtree and `route` is the shell-owned
/// hash route).
package polymorph:todomvc-spike@0.0.1;

world todomvc {
  import polyvisor:surface/dom@0.1.0;
  import polyvisor:surface/events@0.1.0;
  import polyvisor:surface/shell@0.1.0;

  use polyvisor:surface/events@0.1.0.{event};

  /// Build the initial UI (called once, after instantiation).
  export run: func();
  /// Deliver one UI event record.
  export on-event: func(ev: event);
  /// Deliver a route change.
  export on-route: func(route: string);
}

/// Instrumentation world for the backend differential harness and the
/// churn benchmark. Same imports as the app world; the exports drive
/// violation and load scenarios instead of a UI.
///
/// Ordering spec shared by every backend (and by any future native
/// binding): ops apply in call order; a flush boundary falls at the end
/// of each export invocation AND at each guest suspension point; within
/// one boundary, application is atomic with respect to paint. Ops emitted
/// before a trap are applied; the instance is then dead.
world lab {
  import polyvisor:surface/dom@0.1.0;
  import polyvisor:surface/events@0.1.0;
  import polyvisor:surface/shell@0.1.0;

  /// Attempt one violation case (or the legal baseline, id 0). Illegal
  /// cases must trap — at the same call site on every backend.
  export probe: func(id: u32);
  /// Churn scenarios: 1 = create n rows, 2 = update every 10th row,
  /// 3 = clear.
  export bench: func(scenario: u32, n: u32);
}
